{{smaller block|As for the ''tortuosity'' of the body and branches, it maketh nothing to the purpose and point in hand.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Plutarch, p. 562.}}}}

2. A twisting or winding; a bend; a sinuosity.

{{smaller block|Could it be expected . . . that a man so known for impenetrable reticence . . . would all at once frankly unlock his private citadel to an English Editor and a German Hofrath, and not rather deceptively inlock both Editor and Hofrath in the labyrinthic ''tortuosities'' and covered-ways of said citadel?
{{right|''Carlyle'', Sartor Resartus, ii. 10.}}}}

'''tortuous'''<sup>1</sup> (tôr′tṳ̄-us), ''a.'' [< ME. ''tortuous'', ''tortuos'', < OF. ''tortuos'', F. ''tortueux'' = Pr. ''tortuos'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''tortuoso'', < L. ''tortuosus'', full of twists or turns, winding, tortuous, < ''tortus'', a twisting, winding, whirling, a wreath: see ''tort''<sup>3</sup>.] 1. Full of twists or turns; winding; hence, crooked; zigzag. {{smaller|Geometers apply the word specifically to curves of which no two successive portions lie in one plane.}}

{{smaller block|The dragon had grete significacion in hymself, . . . the taile that was so ''tortuouse'' betokened the grete treson of the peple.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 393.}}}}

{{smaller block|An antiquated Manor-house of Elizabethan architecture, with its . . . ''tortuous'' chimneys rising above the surrounding trees.
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, Pref., p. vi.}}}}

2. Oblique: applied in astrology to the six zodiacal signs which ascend most rapidly and obliquely.

{{smaller block|Thise same signes fro the heved of Capricorne unto the ende of Geminis ben cleped ''tortuos'' signes or kroked signes, for they arisen embelif on oure orisonte.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Astrolabe, ii. 28.}}}}

3. Figuratively, circuitous; devious; irregular; crooked: especially in a moral sense.

{{smaller block|Augustus Cæsar was so little able to enter into any artificial forms or ''tortuous'' obscurities of ambitious rhetoric}}

